<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jordan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hashemite Kingdom of Jordan, Transjordan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jordan, officially the Hashemite Kingdom of Jordan, is a country in the Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> region of West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, named for the Jordan River. Ammon, Moab and Edom arose there in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> and the Nabataeans later centred a kingdom on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petra">Petra</a>, before the land passed to the Islamic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphates</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. After the Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arab-revolt">Arab Revolt</a> of 1916, former Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> was partitioned, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Transjordan was established in 1921 as a British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>. Jordan gained independence in 1946. It annexed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bank">West Bank</a> during the 1948 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> war and lost it to Israeli occupation in 1967; it renounced its claim in 1988 and signed a peace treaty with Israel in 1994.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Jordan covers about 89,342 square kilometres and is bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> to the north, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> to the east, Saudi Arabia to the south, and Israel and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bank">West Bank</a> to the west. The Jordan River, flowing into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dead-sea">Dead Sea</a>, runs along the western border within the Jordan Rift Valley, and a short coastline on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-aqaba">Gulf of Aqaba</a> gives access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>. The highlands above the Jordan Valley have a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> climate, while the east and north-east are arid desert. The country is among the most water-scarce in the world, and its two main surface water resources, the Jordan and Yarmuk rivers, are shared with its neighbours.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The oldest evidence of hominid habitation in Jordan is at least 200,000 years old, and the world's oldest known evidence of bread-making comes from a 14,500-year-old Natufian site in the north-eastern desert. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> conquered the Persian Empire in 332 BCE, Transjordan was disputed between the Ptolemies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. The Nabataeans, nomadic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> based south of Edom, used that struggle to found an independent kingdom. It controlled much of the region's trade routes, and its capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petra">Petra</a> flourished in the 1st century CE. The Nabataeans carved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-khazneh">Al-Khazneh</a>, believed to be the mausoleum of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aretas-iv">Aretas IV</a>.</p><p>In 629 Byzantine forces and their Ghassanid allies repelled a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun</a> attack at the Battle of Mu'tah, but the Muslims defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-yarmuk">Battle of the Yarmuk</a> in 636. Rule then passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphates</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arab-revolt">Arab Revolt</a> began on 5 June 1916 from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussein-bin-ali-king-of-hejaz">Sharif Hussein</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and his sons, with the support of Transjordanian tribes, and reached Transjordan in the Battle of Aqaba on 6 July 1917. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> recognised the independence of Transjordan in the Treaty of London, signed on 22 March 1946. On 25 May 1946 it became a kingdom with Abdullah as its first king, and it joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> on 14 December 1955.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amman">Amman</a> is the capital and largest city. The official language is Modern Standard Arabic, and most Jordanians speak Jordanian Arabic. About 95 percent of the population is Muslim, mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a>, and Christians make up about 4 percent. Jordan is a unitary state under a semi-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a>, governed under the constitution of 1952, with a Senate and a House of Representatives, and it is divided into 12 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">governorates</a>. It is a founding member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arab-league">Arab League</a>. Petra is its best-known ancient site, and the country has taken in large numbers of Palestinian and Syrian refugees since 1948.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jordan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
